 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Hermite Data Computation via Bisection](822-hermite-data-computation-via-bisection.md)
-A method to find all edge crossings in an adaptive octree for an implicit function and compute Hermite data (intersection points and normals) using bisection. It refines linear interpolation guesses with bisection to achieve higher precision than standard Marching Cubes, which is essential for dual contouring methods that rely on accurate vertex placement via Quadratic Error Functions (QEF). Unlike [Tree Marcher](7534-tree-marcher.md) which focuses on traversal efficiency, this method focuses on geometric accuracy of the isosurface intersection. Choose this when high-fidelity normals are required for subsequent optimization or rendering.
+A method for extracting precise intersection points and surface normals from an adaptive octree scalar field by refining linear interpolation guesses with iterative bisection. It solves the problem of geometric inaccuracy in adaptive grids where linear interpolation between coarse leaf corners introduces visible jaggedness on curved surfaces, providing the high-precision Hermite data (position and normal) required for Dual Contouring. Unlike [Tree Marcher](7534-tree-marcher.md) or [Tetrahedralization of Octree Cells](9049-tetrahedralization-of-octree-cells.md), which may rely on simpler linear estimates or topological tetrahedralization, this method explicitly refines edge crossings to sub-cell accuracy to ensure manifold quality and sharp feature preservation. Choose this when high-fidelity normals are required for subsequent optimization or rendering.
 
 ### [Tree Marcher](7534-tree-marcher.md)
-The Tree Marcher is a hierarchical ray traversal algorithm that steps a ray through a sparse, hierarchical grid structure (such as an OpenVDB tree) to identify intersections with active nodes. It differs from its siblings by operating directly on the tree topology using a Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) to skip empty space, rather than converting the grid to a mesh or tetrahedral volume. It serves as the core traversal engine for [Hermite Data Computation via Bisection](822-hermite-data-computation-via-bisection.md) and [Tetrahedralization of Octree Cells](9049-tetrahedralization-of-octree-cells.md). Choose this when you need to sample or ray-cast through the volume rather than extract a static mesh.
+The Tree Marcher is a hierarchical ray traversal algorithm that steps a ray through a generic grid structure (such as OpenVDB or NanoVDB) to locate intersections with active nodes. It solves the problem of efficiently navigating adaptive tree structures to find the entry and exit intervals of ray-node intersections, serving as the foundational traversal engine for higher-level operations like zero-crossing detection [G]. It differs from sibling methods by operating on generic grid topologies without special handling for point-grid coordinate mismatches, and from [Hermite Data Computation via Bisection](822-hermite-data-computation-via-bisection.md) by focusing solely on node intersection intervals rather than geometric surface extraction. Choose this when you need to sample or ray-cast through the volume rather than extract a static mesh.
 
 ### [Tetrahedralization of Octree Cells](9049-tetrahedralization-of-octree-cells.md)
-Tetrahedralization of Octree Cells is a method for extracting manifold isosurfaces from adaptive octrees by constructing a dual simplicial partition. It solves the problem of self-intersections and non-manifold vertices inherent in adaptive octrees by ensuring convexity and manifold topology through a dual structure. Unlike Dual Marching Tetrahedra (DMC), which can produce self-intersecting "flaps" due to non-convex primal cells, this method uses the primal octree grid to guarantee convexity, avoiding such artifacts. Compared to Dual Contouring (DC), it samples the function at one higher resolution by including extra samples on edges, faces, and cells, though it is computationally slower. Choose this when a guaranteed manifold mesh is required directly from the octree without iterative optimization.
+Tetrahedralization of Octree Cells is a method for extracting manifold isosurfaces from adaptive octrees by constructing a dual tetrahedral mesh. It solves the problem of self-intersections and non-manifold vertices inherent in standard adaptive grid methods like Dual Marching Cubes (DMC). Unlike DMC, which can produce "flaps" that fold back on themselves due to non-convex cell geometries, this method leverages the convexity of the primal octree grid to ensure a watertight, manifold topology. It is distinct from Hermite Data Computation via Bisection and Tree Marcher by its specific use of dual vertices associated with octree edges, faces, and cells to form a simplicial partition. Choose this when a guaranteed manifold mesh is required directly from the octree without iterative optimization.
 
 ## Background
-The extraction of isosurfaces from hierarchical data structures has evolved from dense grid algorithms like Marching Cubes . The challenge with adaptive octrees is that standard linear interpolation can fail to produce manifold surfaces due to the irregular connectivity of the grid. Early work on OpenVDB introduced hierarchical traversal techniques (Tree Marcher) to efficiently access sparse data . Subsequent research focused on geometric correctness, leading to methods like Tetrahedralization of Octree Cells which use dual structures to enforce manifoldness . Hermite data computation via bisection was developed to provide the high-precision normals required for advanced reconstruction techniques like Dual Contouring, addressing the precision limits of simple linear interpolation . These methods are widely used in scientific visualization and VFX for handling large-scale sparse volumes.
